            Buying One Used
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 14, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="1.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What we checked, and what we have not done.</strong> On
          14 October 2026 we checked the Star Trac treadmills listed by Core Health &amp; Fitness, the
          brand's owner, including its product descriptions and a launch product sheet; Core's own
          history of the brand; trade and finance coverage of the 2010 change of ownership;
          specialist retailer and remanufacturer listings for new and used Star Trac treadmills;
          Zwift's published list of manufacturers with confirmed compatible models; and the US
          Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database, which lists no Star Trac treadmill
          recall (a 2017 recall of Star Trac-branded cable machines did not involve treadmills).
          Figures on this page are the ones on which Core and at least one independent source agree.
          Used and remanufactured prices are the listings we saw that day, not market data. We have
          not tested any Star Trac treadmill.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Is a Star Trac Treadmill a Good Buy for Home? The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#who-owns-star-trac" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Who Owns Star Trac?</a>
          <a href="#lineup" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. What Star Trac Makes Now</a>
          <a href="#buying-new" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Can You Buy a New Star Trac Treadmill for Home?</a>
          <a href="#used-and-refurbished" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Used, Refurbished and Remanufactured Star Trac Treadmills</a>
          <a href="#older-models" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Older Star Trac Models You Will See for Sale</a>
          <a href="#what-to-check" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. What to Check on a Used Star Trac Treadmill</a>
          <a href="#apps-and-subscriptions" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Do Star Trac Treadmills Need a Subscription?</a>
          <a href="#strengths-and-weaknesses" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. What Star Trac Does Well, and Less Well</a>
          <a href="#who-should-buy" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Who Should Buy a Star Trac Treadmill?</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          If you have searched for a Star Trac treadmill, you have probably run on one — at a gym, a
          hotel or a university rec centre — and wondered whether you could have one at home. You
          can, but not in the way you buy most treadmills. Star Trac does not make a home range in the
          ordinary sense: its current treadmills are commercial machines sold to facilities through
          dealers, on quotes rather than price tags. What most home buyers actually find is a used,
          refurbished or remanufactured club unit from a specialist reseller or a gym clear-out. This
          guide covers who owns Star Trac today, what it makes now, which older models you will meet
          for sale, what they cost, and the questions — power, weight, parts and warranty — that
          decide whether a gym treadmill is a good idea in your house.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Is a Star Trac Treadmill a Good Buy for Home? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A Star Trac treadmill is a commercial gym machine, and for most home buyers it
            is a used or remanufactured one; it can be a very good buy if you have the power, the
            space and a way to get it into the room, and if you price it as a machine with little or
            no manufacturer warranty.</strong> Star Trac belongs to Core Health &amp; Fitness, the
            group that also owns StairMaster and the commercial Schwinn and Nautilus lines, and its
            current treadmills are sold to facilities.
          </p>
          <p>
            New, the range runs from the 4TR, aimed at hotels, apartment buildings and residential
            fitness centres, through the 6TR and the new 8TRx to the FreeRunner 10TRx, whose
            cushioned deck, 20% incline and 15 mph top speed make it the flagship. You can buy one
            new for a home through a commercial dealer, but expect a quote, freight delivery and
            commercial warranty terms rather than a consumer checkout.
          </p>
          <p>
            Used, Star Trac treadmills are common, because gyms replace them on a cycle. On the day
            we checked, specialist remanufacturers listed older club models such as the E-TR and
            E-TRx at roughly $3,600 to $3,900 with a one-year parts and on-site labour warranty from
            the reseller, and private sales vary far more. Read our guides to
            <a href="/refurbished-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">refurbished treadmills</a>
            and <a href="/where-to-buy-a-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to buy a used treadmill</a>
            before you shop; the rest of this page covers what is specific to Star Trac.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-owns-star-trac" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Who Owns Star Trac?</h2>
<p>
            Star Trac is owned by Core Health &amp; Fitness. Core's website lists StairMaster,
            Schwinn, Nautilus, Star Trac, Throwdown and Wexer among its brands; the Schwinn and
            Nautilus names there are the commercial lines, separate from the home products sold under
            those names elsewhere. Core sells to commercial facilities, multi-unit housing and
            developers.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A short history</h3>
          <p>
            Accounts of the founding differ. Core's own history of the brand dates Star Trac to
            1979, under a parent company called Unisen, and an older Orange County business
            profile describes the company starting in 1975 with three employees. The company was
            based in Irvine, California. Core credits Star Trac with the Star
            Trac 2000 of 1987, which it calls the first DC-powered commercial treadmill built for
            health clubs, and with the Spinner indoor cycle, which made the brand a fixture of group
            cycling studios.
          </p>
          <p>
            The ownership changed in 2010. Core's history says that Michael Bruno, who had bought
            the StairMaster and commercial Schwinn businesses earlier that year, agreed in July 2010
            to buy a controlling interest in Star Trac's parent company, and that the Star Trac and
            StairMaster businesses were consolidated in 2011. Trade and investment-bank coverage
            at the time described a restructuring and recapitalisation led by Bruno's group. The
            details matter little to a buyer today, with one exception: older Star Trac machines
            were built and warranted by a company that no longer exists in that form, so any
            question about an old unit's warranty or parts should go to Core, with the serial
            number, rather than be assumed.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What that means for home buyers</h3>
          <p>
            Star Trac is a commercial brand first and last. There is no Star Trac home store, no
            consumer price list and no residential warranty to compare with the brands in our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-brands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill brands guide</a>.
            That is not a criticism. It simply means the usual consumer questions — what does it
            cost, what is the warranty, who delivers it — have different answers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Star Trac Makes Now</h2>
<p>
            Core listed four Star Trac treadmills on 14 October 2026. None had a public price; all
            are sold through Core and its dealers on quotation.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Aimed at</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Distinctive feature</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Display options</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">FreeRunner 10TRx</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Clubs, performance training</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">HexDeck aluminium deck on polymer suspension</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">24 in. touch or LED</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">8TRx (new in 2026)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Clubs</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">SoftTrac cushioning, open handrails</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">24 in. touch or LED</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6TR</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Universities, boutique gyms, hotels</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Core's value commercial drive</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">24 or 16 in. touch, or LCD</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4TR</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hotels, apartment and residential fitness centres</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Space-conscious build for unstaffed rooms</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 or 16 in. touch, or LCD</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The FreeRunner 10TRx</h3>
          <p>
            The FreeRunner is the model with the clearest published figures. Core's description and
            specialist retailer listings agree on a maximum incline of 20%, a top speed of 15 mph, a
            5 HP AC motor and a 500 lb user rating. Its HexDeck is an aluminium running deck sitting
            on a hexagonal polymer suspension, which Core describes as giving a track-like,
            cushioned feel. Listings disagree on step-up height and machine weight, so check those
            against the launch sheet if they matter for your room. At least one specialist retailer
            listed a new FreeRunner for sale to the public when we checked.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 8TRx, 6TR and 4TR</h3>
          <p>
            The 8TRx, listed by Core as new in March 2026, is the mainstream club treadmill, with
            Core's SoftTrac triple-cell cushioning and quick-key speed and incline controls. The 6TR
            is pitched as a full commercial treadmill at a lower price for universities, boutique
            gyms and hospitality spaces. The 4TR is the one closest to home use in spirit: Core
            describes it as a space-conscious build for hotels, multi-family housing and residential
            fitness centres, and its 110-volt part numbers carry a 15-amp designation. We did not
            find independent specification sheets for these three that matched Core's, so we leave
            their motor, deck and capacity figures off this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-new" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Can You Buy a New Star Trac Treadmill for Home?</h2>
<p>
            Yes, through a commercial dealer, but it is a different transaction from buying a home
            treadmill. Expect to request a quote rather than see a price, to arrange freight
            delivery, and to pay for installation. Ask the dealer three things before anything else.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>What warranty applies in a home?</strong> Commercial warranties are written for facilities and may treat residential installation differently. Get the terms for your use in writing.</li>
            <li><strong>Who services it where you live?</strong> Commercial service networks are built around gyms in towns and cities. Confirm that a technician will come to a private address, and at what cost after the labour term.</li>
            <li><strong>What power does it need?</strong> Check the electrical requirement in the manual for the exact model and voltage you are buying. Club treadmills often specify a dedicated circuit at a higher rating than a typical household socket; the 4TR's 110-volt part numbers name 15 amps, which is the more home-friendly end of the range.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Weight and access are the other practical limits. Commercial treadmills are long, tall
            and heavy, and they do not fold. Measure stairs, turns and doorways before you order,
            and read our guide to
            <a href="/gym-treadmill-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">putting a gym treadmill in a home</a>,
            which covers floor loading, ceiling height and delivery.
          </p>
          <p>
            For most buyers, the more useful comparison is with heavy home treadmills built for the
            purpose. A <a href="/landice-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Landice L7 or L8</a>
            or a <a href="/spirit-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Spirit XT685</a>
            offers a residential warranty and dealer support at a published price; our
            <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">commercial-grade treadmill roundup</a>
            covers the wider field.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-and-refurbished" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Used, Refurbished and Remanufactured Star Trac Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            This is where most home buyers meet the brand. Gyms replace their cardio floors on a
            cycle, and the machines that come out are bought by specialist resellers, cleaned up or
            rebuilt to varying degrees, and sold on. The words used for that process are not
            standardised, so read the listing for what was actually done.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Grade</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What it usually means</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Typical warranty</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Used, as-is</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Straight out of a facility, perhaps cleaned</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">None</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Refurbished</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Inspected, worn parts replaced as needed, cosmetics tidied</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Reseller's own, often short</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Remanufactured</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stripped down and rebuilt, wear parts replaced, frame refinished</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Reseller's own; one we saw gave one year parts and on-site labour</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What they cost</h3>
          <p>
            On 14 October 2026 one specialist remanufacturer listed a Star Trac E-TR at $3,599 and an
            E-TRx at $3,899, each with a one-year parts and on-site labour warranty and extended
            options. Another reseller listed refurbished 8TR models at $3,895 on sale, both out of
            stock. Private sales and gym liquidations run far lower, with no warranty and the
            collection left to you. Treat those figures as what we saw that day, not as a price
            guide; condition, console and delivery move the number more than the model does.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Is remanufactured worth the premium?</h3>
          <p>
            Often, for a machine this heavy. The price gap between an as-is club treadmill and a
            remanufactured one buys new wear parts, a warranty with on-site labour and, usually,
            delivery by people who move gym equipment for a living. On a 400 lb machine with no
            manufacturer cover, those three things are worth a lot. Our
            <a href="/refurbished-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">refurbished treadmill guide</a>
            sets out what to ask a reseller, and our
            <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new vs used comparison</a>
            weighs the whole decision.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="older-models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Older Star Trac Models You Will See for Sale</h2>
<p>
            Star Trac's model names have changed several times, and used listings use all of them.
            The ones we saw most on resale and remanufacturing sites were these.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>E-TR and E-TRx (E Series).</strong> The most common remanufactured Star Trac treadmills. The E-TR was sold pre-wired for Star Trac's PVS personal entertainment screen, so check whether a listing includes a working screen or just the wiring.</li>
            <li><strong>8TR and earlier 8TRx.</strong> The predecessors of the 2026 8TRx. Check which generation a listing is, because the name has been reused.</li>
            <li><strong>S-TRc (S Series).</strong> Listed by one reseller in Generation 1 and Generation 2 versions, which tells you that the name alone does not identify the machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Pro series.</strong> Older numbered Star Trac Pro treadmills, such as the 5600 Pro, still appear with remanufacturers.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            For any of them, the label on the frame, often near the power inlet, gives the model and serial number. Photograph it, and ask Core or a
            Star Trac service company whether parts for that exact unit are still available before
            you pay. A frame that will outlast you is little use if the console board or the motor
            controller is no longer made. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-repair-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">repair guide</a>
            covers when a repair stops being worth it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. What to Check on a Used Star Trac Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Check the power requirement first, because it decides whether the machine can run in
            your house at all, and then check the treadmill as you would any heavily used machine.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Power.</strong> Find the voltage and amperage on the label or in the manual. If it calls for a 220-volt supply or a dedicated 20-amp circuit, price an electrician before you price the treadmill. Never run a treadmill from an extension cord; our <a href="/treadmill-extension-cord/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cords and circuits guide</a> explains why.</li>
            <li><strong>Hours and history.</strong> Ask where the machine was used and for how long. A hotel treadmill and a 24-hour gym treadmill of the same age have lived very different lives. If the console records hours or miles, note them.</li>
            <li><strong>Belt and deck.</strong> Look for fraying, glazing and a deck that feels dry or rough under the belt. On a refurbished unit, ask whether either was replaced.</li>
            <li><strong>Motor under load.</strong> Run it at your real speed, at incline, for ten minutes. Listen for labouring, surging or a hot smell.</li>
            <li><strong>Incline.</strong> Take it through the full range and back. Commercial incline motors work hard.</li>
            <li><strong>Console.</strong> Press every button. A dead key on a club console can mean a part that is expensive or no longer available.</li>
            <li><strong>Delivery.</strong> Confirm how it gets into your room. Club treadmills often weigh several hundred pounds and do not fold.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            goes through each step in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="apps-and-subscriptions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Do Star Trac Treadmills Need a Subscription?</h2>
<p>
            No. Star Trac treadmills are built for gyms, where nobody signs up to a platform to use
            the cardio floor, so every console works on its own. The current touch consoles add
            onboard HIIT programming, wireless mirroring of a personal device, built-in entertainment
            apps and NFC tap-to-pair for Apple and Samsung Galaxy watches. The LED and LCD consoles
            are simpler.
          </p>
          <p>
            Zwift's list of manufacturers with confirmed compatible models includes Star Trac, and
            Zwift says compatibility varies by model and production year. In practice that means the
            console generation matters: an older E-TR or 8TR may have no Bluetooth at all. If you
            want to run on Zwift with a used Star Trac, plan on a foot pod unless the seller can show
            the console pairing. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift treadmill roundup</a>
            and <a href="/treadmill-foot-pod-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">foot pod guide</a>
            cover both routes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strengths-and-weaknesses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What Star Trac Does Well, and Less Well</h2>
<h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Does well</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Durability by design.</strong> These are machines built for gyms, where they run for hours a day; a used one bought at the right price can outlast a new budget treadmill.</li>
            <li><strong>Plain, subscription-free consoles.</strong> Nothing stops working when an app changes.</li>
            <li><strong>Plenty of used supply.</strong> Gym replacement cycles keep remanufacturers stocked, which gives buyers choice.</li>
            <li><strong>The FreeRunner.</strong> 20% incline and a cushioned deck are unusual on any treadmill.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Does less well, for home buyers</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>No consumer route.</strong> No home store, no published prices, no residential warranty.</li>
            <li><strong>Size, weight and power.</strong> Non-folding, heavy, and often wanting a dedicated circuit.</li>
            <li><strong>Parts for old models.</strong> Availability varies by model and age; check before you buy.</li>
            <li><strong>Little or no warranty used.</strong> Unless a remanufacturer provides one.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy a Star Trac Treadmill?</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy a remanufactured Star Trac if</strong> you want a gym-grade machine for heavy
            use, you have a ground-floor or easily reached room with the right power, and you are
            buying from a reseller who gives a written warranty with on-site labour and delivers
            the machine into place.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy an as-is used Star Trac if</strong> it is cheap, you have seen it run at your
            speed and incline, you have confirmed parts are available, and you can move a very heavy
            machine safely. Price it as a machine that may need a technician.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy new through a dealer if</strong> you specifically want the FreeRunner's deck
            and 20% incline, or you are equipping a building's gym rather than a home.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy a heavy home treadmill instead if</strong> you want a published price, a
            residential warranty and someone to call. The
            <a href="/landice-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Landice</a> and
            <a href="/spirit-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Spirit</a> brand guides
            and our <a href="/matrix-t5x-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Matrix T5x review</a>,
            another club treadmill most buyers meet second-hand, are the places to compare.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who makes Star Trac treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Star Trac is owned by Core Health and Fitness, which also owns StairMaster and the commercial Schwinn, Nautilus and Throwdown lines. Star Trac began in California under a parent called Unisen; Core dates it to 1979, though another account says 1975. Michael Bruno’s group took control in 2010, and the business was combined with StairMaster in 2011.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Star Trac make home treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in the usual sense. Its current treadmills, the FreeRunner 10TRx, 8TRx, 6TR and 4TR, are commercial machines sold to facilities through dealers on quotation. The 4TR is aimed at hotels, apartment buildings and residential fitness centres. You can buy one new for a home through a dealer, but most home buyers find used or remanufactured units.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much is a used Star Trac treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on condition and seller. On 14 October 2026 one remanufacturer listed an E-TR at $3,599 and an E-TRx at $3,899 with a one-year parts and on-site labour warranty, and another listed refurbished 8TR models at $3,895. Private and gym-liquidation sales run much lower but usually come without warranty or delivery.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a Star Trac treadmill run on a household outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check the label and manual for the exact model and voltage. Many club treadmills call for a dedicated circuit, some at 20 amps or 220 volts, which most rooms do not have. The 4TR’s 110-volt part numbers name 15 amps. Do not use an extension cord, and price an electrician before buying if the requirement does not match your wiring.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the Star Trac FreeRunner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The FreeRunner 10TRx is Star Trac’s flagship treadmill. It has an aluminium HexDeck running deck on a hexagonal polymer suspension, which Core describes as cushioned and track-like. Core and specialist retailers agree on a 20% maximum incline, 15 mph top speed, 5 HP AC motor and 500 lb user rating. It is sold through dealers rather than at a public price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Star Trac treadmills work with Zwift?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some do. Zwift lists Star Trac among manufacturers with confirmed compatible models but says compatibility varies by model and production year. Current touch consoles have wireless connectivity; older E Series and 8TR consoles may have no Bluetooth. With an older unit, plan to use a foot pod, which works with any treadmill once calibrated.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Star Trac Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">A gym treadmill at home can be an excellent buy. These are the mistakes that turn it into an expensive problem.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying before checking the power requirement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Club treadmills often need a dedicated circuit, sometimes at 20 amps or 220 volts. Find the requirement on the label or in the manual first, and get an electrician’s quote if your room does not match. An extension cord or shared circuit is not an acceptable workaround on a machine drawing this much current.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the model name alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Star Trac reused names across generations, and resellers list Generation 1 and 2 versions of the same model. Ask for a photograph of the frame label, and check the console and parts availability for that exact unit. Two machines called 8TR can be years and several console generations apart.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the manufacturer warranty comes with it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Commercial warranties are written for the original facility, and a used unit should be treated as having none. The only cover you can count on is what a reseller gives you in writing. Compare an as-is price with a remanufactured price that includes a parts and on-site labour warranty.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting how it gets into the room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Club treadmills are long, heavy and do not fold. Measure every doorway, turn and stair on the route, check the floor, and confirm whether delivery is to the kerb or into the room. A machine that cannot reach the basement is not a bargain at any price.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

